INT. DANCE MARATHON HALL - NIGHT (1932)

A cavernous ballroom, plank floor, paper streamers gone limp. Hour 487. A banner reads LAST COUPLE STANDING WINS $1,000. The couples have gone to their cots. A gramophone scratches out one slow waltz to an empty floor.

EVELYN PRATT, 20s, in a tattered satin dress, shoes in her hand, sways alone in the middle of the boards. Eyes closed.

EVELYN
(to the empty hall)
Anybody got a cigarette?

No answer. Gramophone crackle.

EVELYN (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Four hundred eighty-seven hours. And the only thing that moves is the record.

A cot creaks in the shadows. A match flares. JACK HOLLISTER, 20s, in a wilted white shirt, pushes himself off a bench and shuffles into the light, a cigarette between his lips.

JACK
Here.

He offers it. She takes a drag. Gives it back.

EVELYN
Thanks. You were sleeping.

JACK
I was resting my eyes.

EVELYN
In a dance marathon.

JACK
It's called pacing.

She laughs. It wakes him a little. He sets the cigarette on the edge of the gramophone and holds out one hand.

JACK (CONT'D)
Fifteen minutes left on the clock.

EVELYN
Then dance with me.

JACK
I thought you'd never ask.

She drops her shoes. Takes his hand. They begin to turn, slow, a little off the beat.

EVELYN
If we win, what will you do with a thousand dollars?

JACK
Buy a hat.

EVELYN
That's all?

JACK
A nice hat.

EVELYN
And me?

JACK
You'd get a better hat.

They both begin to say the same line.

EVELYN
I'd dance another four hundred hours for you.

JACK ^
I'd dance another four hundred hours for you.

They stop. Both startled. Then, softly, they laugh.

EVELYN (CONT'D)
Don't promise. Just hold on.

He holds her. The gramophone skips and keeps going.
